// #pragma once

// THE ONE PLACE AN EQUIRECTANGULAR SKY IS READ.
//
// WHY THIS IS A SHARED TEXT AND NOT TWO COPIES. The HDR environment is built from ONE panorama by TWO
// compute programs that must agree pixel for pixel about what that panorama says in a direction:
// PanoramaToCubemap writes the radiance cube the background is drawn from and the GGX prefilter
// convolves, and DiffuseIrradiance integrates the SAME panorama into the irradiance cube every lit
// surface reads. Two copies of one mapping is the middle-link-drops-a-property shape this engine has
// paid for repeatedly, and the copies cannot be kept honest by review, because each reads correct alone.
//
// THE SKY'S AUTHORED LOOK IS NOT APPLIED HERE ANY MORE. It turned and scaled the panorama at the bake,
// which made every rotation value a new bake; it is now applied where the cubes are sampled
// (Common/SkyLook.glslh), and the cubes this text feeds are the file as authored.

// The panorama texel a world DIRECTION reads.
vec2 PanoramaSampleUV(vec3 d)
{
    const float kPi = 3.14159265358979323846;

    float phi   = atan( d.z, d.x );
    // CLAMPED: a direction that arrives a bit longer than unit (the cube-face vectors are normalized,
    // but a caller's need not be) makes acos return NaN, and one NaN texel poisons every mip the
    // prefilter builds from it.
    // `float(...)` around the bounds, not bare literals: this text also compiles as C++, where -1.0 is
    // a DOUBLE and no glm::clamp overload takes (float, double, double).
    float theta = acos( clamp( d.y, float( -1.0 ), float( 1.0 ) ) );

    return vec2( phi / ( 2.0 * kPi ) + 0.5, theta / kPi );
}
